WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.982
Task statement 4.4.

2
00:00:01.982 --> 00:00:07.519
Implement validation, retry, and feedback loops for extraction quality.

3
00:00:07.519 --> 00:00:17.033
An invoice's line items add up to 410 but Claude extracted a total of 401; your loop must catch it and fix it, or flag it.

4
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:22.038
A record can match the schema perfectly and still be wrong.

5
00:00:22.038 --> 00:00:27.475
The line items might not add up to the total, or a date might fall before the order date.

6
00:00:27.475 --> 00:00:29.735
So validate in code after extraction.

7
00:00:29.735 --> 00:00:37.249
When something fails, send Claude the exact problem, like a teacher's margin note, rather than a bare request to try again.

8
00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:41.718
A good retry tells Claude what failed and where.

9
00:00:41.718 --> 00:00:46.321
Line items sum to four hundred ten, but the total says four hundred one.

10
00:00:46.321 --> 00:00:48.495
Please re-read the totals section.

11
00:00:48.495 --> 00:00:51.052
That fixes misreadings and format slips.

12
00:00:51.052 --> 00:00:56.486
But if the value is simply not in the document, no number of retries will produce it.

13
00:00:56.486 --> 00:00:58.660
Return null, flag it, and move on.

14
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.672
Some fields exist to make problems visible.

15
00:01:02.672 --> 00:01:10.266
Capture the total printed on the document and the total calculated from line items, and set a conflict flag when they differ.

16
00:01:10.266 --> 00:01:14.518
For review style outputs, record which pattern triggered each finding.

17
00:01:14.518 --> 00:01:21.991
Later, when people dismiss findings, you can group them by pattern and see exactly which rule is producing false positives.

18
00:01:23.391 --> 00:01:29.181
In the extraction pipeline, the validator catches a total that does not match its line items.

19
00:01:29.181 --> 00:01:33.912
The retry includes the precise numbers, and Claude corrects a misread digit.

20
00:01:33.912 --> 00:01:39.453
Another document has no due date at all, so the pipeline stops retrying and records null.

21
00:01:39.453 --> 00:01:44.745
When the source itself is inconsistent, both totals are kept and flagged for a human.

22
00:01:46.145 --> 00:01:48.511
Retry questions hinge on two ideas.

23
00:01:48.511 --> 00:01:53.107
A good retry carries the specific error, never a blind resubmission.

24
00:01:53.107 --> 00:01:57.569
And retries only help when the answer is actually in the document.

25
00:01:57.569 --> 00:02:00.205
For missing data, stop and return null.

26
00:02:00.205 --> 00:02:05.409
For genuine conflicts, keep both values and flag them so a person can decide.

27
00:02:06.809 --> 00:02:10.905
Build a loop: extract, validate, and retry with the exact error.

28
00:02:10.905 --> 00:02:15.577
Know when to stop, because missing data cannot be retried into existence.

29
00:02:15.577 --> 00:02:23.129
Design fields that expose conflicts and patterns, so you can measure and fix the prompt over time instead of guessing.

